{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Recursive Algorithm Correctness.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Recursive Algorithm Correctness.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"recursive algorithm correctness recursive algorithm correctness","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Recursive Algorithm Correctness.md","fileName":"Recursive Algorithm Correctness.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Recursive Algorithm Correctness","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-16 22:54\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Proof Methods and Recursive Definitions]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Proof Methods and Recursive Definitions","Strong Induction","FoundationsOfComputation_2.3.2.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Recursive Algorithm Correctness.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Recursive Algorithm Correctness.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"recursive algorithm correctness recursive algorithm correctness","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Recursive Algorithm Correctness.md","fileName":"Recursive Algorithm Correctness.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Recursive Algorithm Correctness","heading":"Recursive Algorithm Correctness","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"An induction proof can mirror the execution of a recursive algorithm. The induction base case verifies the branch that returns without another call, while the inductive case assumes smaller recursive calls are correct and proves that the surrounding computation produces the correct larger result.\n\nThe proof also reveals the termination structure: every recursive call must move toward a base case. [[Strong Induction]] is especially useful when a call operates on substructures whose sizes can be any value below the original size.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Proof Methods and Recursive Definitions","Strong Induction","